## Sample Solution: HashMap

### Core Classes
```
class Entry<K, V> { key: K; value: V; hash: int; next: Entry<K, V> }

class HashMap<K, V> {
    static DEFAULT_CAPACITY = 16; static LOAD_FACTOR = 0.75
    buckets: Entry<K, V>[]; size: int; capacity: int

    private hash(key): int { h = key.hashCode(); return h ^ (h >>> 16) }
    private getBucketIndex(hash): int { return hash & (capacity - 1) }

    put(key, value): V { index = getBucketIndex(hash(key)); traverse chain, update or insert; resize if needed }
    get(key): V { index = getBucketIndex(hash(key)); traverse chain; return value or null }
    remove(key): V { index = getBucketIndex(hash(key)); unlink from chain; return value }
    private resize(): void { double capacity; rehash all entries }
}
```

### Key Design Decisions
1. Power-of-2 capacity allows bitwise AND for fast bucket index
2. Hash spreading (XOR with right shift) distributes keys evenly
3. Chaining handles collisions simply
4. Resize doubles capacity and rehashes (amortized O(1))
5. New entries at head of chain for O(1) insertion
6. Generics allow type-safe usage

